oles and duties of the Levitical priesthood, establishing religious order."}], "places": [{"name": "Canaan, Israel", "role": "Promised Land"}, {"name": "Mount Sinai, Egypt", "role": "Sacred site"}, {"name": "Babylon, Iraq", "role": "Exile location"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Book of Numbers", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "book-of-numbers"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q43099", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Numbers"], "tags": ["religious text", "Other Sacred & Wisdom Texts", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Book of Numbers left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 126 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Book of Numbers profoundly shaped Jewish law and identity, influencing millions through its teachings on faith and community organization throughout history.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-17T01:39:40Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27659859342", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "The Book of Numbers is the fourth book of the Hebrew Bible and the fourth of five books of the Jewish Torah. The book has a long and complex history; its final form is possibly due to a Priestly redaction of a Yahwistic source made sometime in the early Persian period. The name of the book comes from the two censuses taken of the Israelites.", "newValue": "The Book of Numbers stands as a critical text within the Hebrew Bible, likely compiled around the 5th century BCE during the Persian period, and serves as the fourth book of the Torah. Its significance lies in its dual function as a historical record of the Israelites' journey from Mount Sinai to the Promised Land and as a foundational document for Jewish law and